1
00:00:01,240 --> 00:00:02,763
Imagine an auction house.

2
00:00:02,783 --> 00:00:03,720
The lights dim,

3
00:00:03,740 --> 00:00:04,784
the hammer rises,

4
00:00:04,804 --> 00:00:06,273
and the auctioneer says:

5
00:00:06,293 --> 00:00:07,230
Lot number one.

6
00:00:07,250 --> 00:00:08,028
Planet Earth.

7
00:00:08,048 --> 00:00:10,261
Everything on it, and everyone.

8
00:00:11,371 --> 00:00:12,191
What would you bid?

9
00:00:12,211 --> 00:00:13,381
A million dollars?

10
00:00:13,431 --> 00:00:14,191
A billion?

11
00:00:14,211 --> 00:00:16,062
Every coin in every pocket?

12
00:00:16,632 --> 00:00:19,022
People have actually tried to add it all up.

13
00:00:19,242 --> 00:00:21,042
Every home, every company,

14
00:00:21,062 --> 00:00:22,342
every bar of gold.

15
00:00:22,622 --> 00:00:25,587
The total is more than five hundred trillion dollars.

16
00:00:26,767 --> 00:00:28,367
But here's the strange part.

17
00:00:28,587 --> 00:00:30,167
That giant number leaves out the

18
00:00:30,187 --> 00:00:32,077
most valuable thing on the planet.

19
00:00:32,307 --> 00:00:35,115
And it isn't gold, or oil, or land.

20
00:00:36,305 --> 00:00:37,555
By the end of this video,

21
00:00:37,575 --> 00:00:40,025
you'll know the price tag on the whole world,

22
00:00:40,235 --> 00:00:41,705
what that number is missing,

23
00:00:41,865 --> 00:00:43,402
and why the honest answer might

24
00:00:43,422 --> 00:00:44,729
be no price at all.

25
00:00:45,329 --> 00:00:48,131
So, how much is the whole world actually worth?

26
00:00:55,121 --> 00:00:55,971
Chapter one.

27
00:00:55,991 --> 00:00:57,187
Everything we own.

28
00:00:57,877 --> 00:00:59,587
Start with a simple question.

29
00:00:59,797 --> 00:01:01,857
If you sold everything people own,

30
00:01:01,877 --> 00:01:02,930
what would you get?

31
00:01:03,520 --> 00:01:07,182
Every year, the bank UBS adds it up: houses, savings,

32
00:01:07,202 --> 00:01:08,593
shares and pensions,

33
00:01:08,613 --> 00:01:10,173
minus every debt.

34
00:01:10,773 --> 00:01:13,183
Add up the figures in its latest report,

35
00:01:13,323 --> 00:01:15,873
and people owned about five hundred and eighteen

36
00:01:15,893 --> 00:01:18,713
trillion dollars at the end of twenty twenty-five.

37
00:01:19,123 --> 00:01:22,002
And that's only the fifty-six markets it tracks.

38
00:01:23,262 --> 00:01:25,752
Five hundred trillion is hard to picture.

39
00:01:25,972 --> 00:01:27,252
So here's a trick.

40
00:01:27,472 --> 00:01:29,732
If you counted one dollar every second,

41
00:01:29,802 --> 00:01:32,213
you would need about sixteen million years.

42
00:01:33,403 --> 00:01:34,713
Shared out evenly,

43
00:01:34,833 --> 00:01:36,786
that would be about a hundred and thirty

44
00:01:36,806 --> 00:01:39,393
thousand dollars for every adult in those markets.

45
00:01:39,703 --> 00:01:41,213
But it isn't shared evenly,

46
00:01:41,293 --> 00:01:43,277
and most people own far less.

47
00:01:43,857 --> 00:01:44,997
In just one year,

48
00:01:45,107 --> 00:01:47,617
that pile grew by almost eleven percent,

49
00:01:47,787 --> 00:01:51,010
and nearly one million people became dollar millionaires.

50
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:52,770
So where is all this wealth?

51
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,074
Mostly, it's right under our feet.

52
00:01:55,654 --> 00:01:57,778
The world's buildings and land are worth

53
00:01:57,798 --> 00:02:00,784
about three hundred and ninety-three trillion dollars.

54
00:02:01,044 --> 00:02:03,319
Almost three quarters of that is homes.

55
00:02:04,519 --> 00:02:07,599
Real estate is the largest store of wealth on Earth.

56
00:02:08,019 --> 00:02:10,109
It's worth more than all the world's shares,

57
00:02:10,149 --> 00:02:12,101
bonds and gold put together.

58
00:02:13,291 --> 00:02:14,111
But wait.

59
00:02:14,231 --> 00:02:15,608
Isn't the real treasure of the

60
00:02:15,628 --> 00:02:17,170
world supposed to be gold?

61
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:19,190
Chapter two.

62
00:02:19,210 --> 00:02:20,298
The gold myth.

63
00:02:20,968 --> 00:02:23,088
Picture all the gold ever mined,

64
00:02:23,118 --> 00:02:24,628
in all of human history.

65
00:02:24,928 --> 00:02:27,129
How big a pile do you think that makes?

66
00:02:27,709 --> 00:02:28,479
A mountain?

67
00:02:28,499 --> 00:02:29,259
A city?

68
00:02:29,509 --> 00:02:32,259
It's about two hundred and twenty-four thousand tonnes.

69
00:02:33,249 --> 00:02:34,819
Melt it into one cube,

70
00:02:34,859 --> 00:02:36,287
and each side would be only

71
00:02:36,307 --> 00:02:38,259
about twenty-two and a half metres.

72
00:02:38,559 --> 00:02:40,169
It would fit on a football pitch,

73
00:02:40,189 --> 00:02:41,865
with room to play around it.

74
00:02:43,065 --> 00:02:44,573
Gold is so dense that this

75
00:02:44,593 --> 00:02:46,695
one cube holds every wedding ring,

76
00:02:46,865 --> 00:02:50,518
every coin, and every bar in every central bank vault.

77
00:02:51,098 --> 00:02:52,858
Almost half of it is jewellery.

78
00:02:53,038 --> 00:02:55,542
Only about a fifth sits in central banks.

79
00:02:56,122 --> 00:02:58,532
At around three thousand nine hundred dollars an ounce,

80
00:02:58,682 --> 00:03:01,441
it's worth about twenty-eight trillion dollars.

81
00:03:02,641 --> 00:03:03,901
That sounds huge.

82
00:03:04,061 --> 00:03:06,851
But it's only about one twentieth of what people own.

83
00:03:07,051 --> 00:03:08,747
The idea that the world's wealth

84
00:03:08,767 --> 00:03:10,101
is gold in a vault?

85
00:03:10,241 --> 00:03:11,182
That's a myth.

86
00:03:12,382 --> 00:03:13,802
So if wealth isn't gold,

87
00:03:13,822 --> 00:03:14,942
maybe it's money.

88
00:03:15,182 --> 00:03:17,787
Surely there's a giant pile of cash somewhere?

89
00:03:18,967 --> 00:03:19,827
Chapter three.

90
00:03:19,847 --> 00:03:21,001
Where is the money?

91
00:03:21,721 --> 00:03:23,611
Here's a question that sounds silly.

92
00:03:23,801 --> 00:03:26,125
How much of your money actually exists?

93
00:03:26,705 --> 00:03:28,205
Take the United States.

94
00:03:28,525 --> 00:03:30,415
In September twenty twenty-six,

95
00:03:30,485 --> 00:03:34,165
Americans held about twenty-three trillion dollars in cash,

96
00:03:34,235 --> 00:03:35,927
bank accounts and savings.

97
00:03:36,517 --> 00:03:38,600
But only about two point four trillion

98
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:40,607
of it was real paper and coins.

99
00:03:40,897 --> 00:03:42,701
Roughly one dollar in ten.

100
00:03:43,921 --> 00:03:45,801
The other nine are just numbers.

101
00:03:46,001 --> 00:03:47,681
Digits in bank computers,

102
00:03:47,701 --> 00:03:49,370
that move when you tap a card.

103
00:03:49,960 --> 00:03:51,242
Even the money in your bank

104
00:03:51,262 --> 00:03:52,660
isn't sitting in a drawer.

105
00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:54,920
Banks lend most of it out again,

106
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:57,701
and that loan becomes someone else's deposit.

107
00:03:58,691 --> 00:04:00,411
And wealth works the same way.

108
00:04:00,701 --> 00:04:03,119
A house is worth five hundred thousand dollars

109
00:04:03,139 --> 00:04:05,663
only because someone would pay that today.

110
00:04:06,243 --> 00:04:07,632
If everyone tried to sell their

111
00:04:07,652 --> 00:04:09,013
home on the same morning,

112
00:04:09,043 --> 00:04:10,443
prices would crash.

113
00:04:10,633 --> 00:04:12,203
The wealth wouldn't move anywhere.

114
00:04:12,363 --> 00:04:13,802
It would simply vanish.

115
00:04:14,982 --> 00:04:16,952
So worth isn't a pile of stuff.

116
00:04:17,172 --> 00:04:18,102
It's a price.

117
00:04:18,322 --> 00:04:20,183
And some of the most valuable things on

118
00:04:20,203 --> 00:04:22,050
Earth have no price tag at all.

119
00:04:23,230 --> 00:04:24,080
Chapter four.

120
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:25,199
The free stuff.

121
00:04:25,899 --> 00:04:28,039
What does the planet do for us every day,

122
00:04:28,129 --> 00:04:29,747
without ever sending a bill?

123
00:04:30,387 --> 00:04:32,667
Forests clean the air and hold the soil.

124
00:04:32,887 --> 00:04:34,447
Wetlands filter water.

125
00:04:34,567 --> 00:04:36,657
Bees carry pollen to our crops.

126
00:04:36,827 --> 00:04:38,867
Oceans feed billions of people.

127
00:04:39,457 --> 00:04:40,707
In twenty fourteen,

128
00:04:40,877 --> 00:04:43,802
a team led by the ecologist Robert Costanza tried

129
00:04:43,822 --> 00:04:47,006
to estimate what all these services are worth.

130
00:04:47,586 --> 00:04:51,326
Their answer: about one hundred and twenty-five trillion dollars,

131
00:04:51,346 --> 00:04:52,649
every single year.

132
00:04:53,829 --> 00:04:54,619
At the time,

133
00:04:54,639 --> 00:04:56,759
the whole world economy produced about

134
00:04:56,779 --> 00:04:58,979
seventy-five trillion dollars a year.

135
00:04:59,419 --> 00:05:01,819
Nature was doing more work than all of us,

136
00:05:01,839 --> 00:05:02,603
for free.

137
00:05:03,813 --> 00:05:06,893
And when forests are cut down or wetlands drained,

138
00:05:07,013 --> 00:05:08,493
we lose part of that work.

139
00:05:08,893 --> 00:05:10,973
The same study put the loss at somewhere

140
00:05:10,993 --> 00:05:13,753
between four and twenty trillion dollars a year.

141
00:05:14,733 --> 00:05:15,795
And none of it shows up

142
00:05:15,815 --> 00:05:17,293
in the five hundred trillion.

143
00:05:17,593 --> 00:05:18,973
Nobody owns the rain,

144
00:05:18,993 --> 00:05:20,627
so no bank can count it.

145
00:05:21,207 --> 00:05:22,067
It's an estimate,

146
00:05:22,087 --> 00:05:23,887
not a price anyone pays.

147
00:05:24,117 --> 00:05:25,957
But it raises a bigger question.

148
00:05:26,227 --> 00:05:28,637
If the land and the sea aren't fully counted,

149
00:05:28,657 --> 00:05:29,939
what else is missing?

150
00:05:31,129 --> 00:05:32,069
Chapter five.

151
00:05:32,109 --> 00:05:33,686
The biggest asset is you.

152
00:05:34,386 --> 00:05:36,161
What if the most valuable thing on

153
00:05:36,181 --> 00:05:38,490
Earth is watching this video right now?

154
00:05:39,060 --> 00:05:41,400
The World Bank measures wealth a different way.

155
00:05:41,640 --> 00:05:42,730
It counts buildings,

156
00:05:42,750 --> 00:05:44,230
machines and nature.

157
00:05:44,450 --> 00:05:46,074
But it also counts people.

158
00:05:46,674 --> 00:05:48,465
Not people for sale, of course.

159
00:05:48,485 --> 00:05:52,139
It counts everything people will earn over their working lives.

160
00:05:52,159 --> 00:05:54,917
Their skills, their health, their education.

161
00:05:55,497 --> 00:05:56,477
Add it all up,

162
00:05:56,637 --> 00:05:59,568
and in twenty eighteen the world's total wealth came to

163
00:05:59,588 --> 00:06:03,017
about one thousand one hundred and fifty-two trillion dollars.

164
00:06:03,317 --> 00:06:04,805
More than a quadrillion.

165
00:06:06,015 --> 00:06:07,892
And sixty-four percent of that,

166
00:06:07,912 --> 00:06:09,030
almost two thirds,

167
00:06:09,050 --> 00:06:10,168
was human capital.

168
00:06:10,188 --> 00:06:10,749
Us.

169
00:06:11,949 --> 00:06:13,926
That's why schools and hospitals matter

170
00:06:13,946 --> 00:06:15,749
so much to a country's wealth.

171
00:06:16,099 --> 00:06:18,529
People aren't just a cost on the balance sheet.

172
00:06:18,699 --> 00:06:19,829
They are most of it.

173
00:06:20,409 --> 00:06:24,069
Every nurse, every farmer,

174
00:06:24,089 --> 00:06:25,341
every student learning something new is part

175
00:06:25,361 --> 00:06:26,639
of the biggest asset on the planet.

176
00:06:26,989 --> 00:06:27,809
Next to that,

177
00:06:27,929 --> 00:06:29,866
all the gold is small change.

178
00:06:30,846 --> 00:06:32,666
So now we have a few answers.

179
00:06:32,746 --> 00:06:35,076
But what if we tried to price the planet itself?

180
00:06:35,216 --> 00:06:36,293
Rock and all?

181
00:06:37,473 --> 00:06:38,413
Chapter six.

182
00:06:38,433 --> 00:06:39,677
Pricing a planet.

183
00:06:40,367 --> 00:06:42,427
Let's do the maths, blueprint style.

184
00:06:42,597 --> 00:06:44,715
How much would Earth fetch at the scrapyard?

185
00:06:45,305 --> 00:06:48,485
Earth weighs about six trillion trillion kilograms.

186
00:06:48,745 --> 00:06:50,815
And roughly a third of that is iron,

187
00:06:50,865 --> 00:06:52,619
most of it locked in the core.

188
00:06:53,209 --> 00:06:55,999
That's about two billion trillion tonnes of iron.

189
00:06:56,289 --> 00:06:59,393
And iron ore sells for about ninety dollars a tonne.

190
00:07:00,383 --> 00:07:01,383
Multiply,

191
00:07:01,453 --> 00:07:04,753
and you get around one hundred and seventy sextillion dollars.

192
00:07:05,043 --> 00:07:08,721
That's over three hundred million times everything people own.

193
00:07:09,901 --> 00:07:11,344
Of course, that's nonsense.

194
00:07:11,364 --> 00:07:13,060
Nobody can dig out the core,

195
00:07:13,080 --> 00:07:15,230
and nobody could buy that much iron.

196
00:07:15,250 --> 00:07:17,097
It's like pricing a painting by

197
00:07:17,117 --> 00:07:18,910
the weight of its paint.

198
00:07:19,490 --> 00:07:20,520
In twenty eleven,

199
00:07:20,540 --> 00:07:22,534
the astronomer Greg Laughlin made a

200
00:07:22,554 --> 00:07:24,770
playful formula for pricing planets.

201
00:07:25,120 --> 00:07:26,420
His estimate for Earth:

202
00:07:26,700 --> 00:07:28,883
about five quadrillion dollars.

203
00:07:29,463 --> 00:07:30,613
So which number is right?

204
00:07:30,653 --> 00:07:31,783
Here's the twist.

205
00:07:31,933 --> 00:07:32,603
Neither.

206
00:07:32,643 --> 00:07:34,863
A price needs a buyer and a seller.

207
00:07:35,153 --> 00:07:37,023
And there's nobody to sell Earth to,

208
00:07:37,043 --> 00:07:38,473
and nowhere else to go.

209
00:07:39,683 --> 00:07:41,409
No market, no price.

210
00:07:42,589 --> 00:07:44,942
So, how much is the whole world worth?

211
00:07:45,532 --> 00:07:46,992
Count what people own,

212
00:07:47,012 --> 00:07:49,592
and it's more than five hundred trillion dollars.

213
00:07:49,832 --> 00:07:51,322
Count the people themselves,

214
00:07:51,342 --> 00:07:52,852
and it's over a quadrillion.

215
00:07:53,212 --> 00:07:54,772
Count nature's free work,

216
00:07:54,812 --> 00:07:57,539
and add more than a hundred trillion, every year.

217
00:07:58,719 --> 00:08:00,080
But the planet itself?

218
00:08:00,100 --> 00:08:01,196
There's no auction,

219
00:08:01,216 --> 00:08:02,205
no second Earth,

220
00:08:02,225 --> 00:08:03,108
and no bidder.

221
00:08:03,128 --> 00:08:05,285
The only honest price is priceless.

222
00:08:05,305 --> 00:08:06,347
Not as a slogan,

223
00:08:06,367 --> 00:08:09,478
but because there's nothing to compare it to.

224
00:08:10,668 --> 00:08:11,858
Back at that auction,

225
00:08:11,898 --> 00:08:13,478
the hammer never falls.

226
00:08:13,678 --> 00:08:15,151
Because everyone in the room is

227
00:08:15,171 --> 00:08:16,809
already standing on the lot.

228
00:08:17,389 --> 00:08:19,139
If you enjoyed this,

229
00:08:19,159 --> 00:08:20,729
give it a like,

230
00:08:21,009 --> 00:08:22,389
and subscribe to Whyverse.

231
00:08:22,469 --> 00:08:24,159
And for shorter stories,

232
00:08:24,179 --> 00:08:24,633
find us on Instagram and TikTok.

